  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nYesterday was Independence Day \u2014 a structural 50% revenue depressor \u2014 but Port Washington's grooming collapse (down 40% core, zero groomer-days worked) and add-on attach cliff (50% vs 75%) signal operational concern beneath the holiday noise. You had only 2 bather hours when trailing average is 4.8; that staffing gap cascaded into weak upsell discipline. Retail held (+39%), so front-of-house was engaged, but grooming capacity dried up. Month-to-date Port is tracking 15.7% below DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014real performance loss, not calendar. That said, year-to-date you're up 49% revenue and 50% appointments vs last year; that's the win to build on. One bright spot: Port's 12+ week visit-cadence bucket is shrinking (15.5% \u2192 13.3%), your leading indicator of retention recovery.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Visit-cadence drift is moving the wrong way\u2014your 12+ week bucket grew from 10.2% to 12.6%\u2014which means customers are stretching intervals and you're experiencing stealth churn. Yesterday you had one groomer (Julia B) producing $245 against a trailing-30d baseline of $402/groomer/day, so she ran cold despite solid core revenue. More troubling: you have zero groomer-days scheduled today and only five scheduled across the next seven days. That's below Port's capacity ratio and unsustainable for growth. Hicksville's MTD is ahead of DOW-adjusted expectation (+9.8%), so it's not performance\u2014it's coverage.\n\nThe pattern across both stores is groomer-days under target. Port is at 3.6/day (down from prior-month 4.4); Hicksville at 2.4/day (flat to prior-month 2.2). Your $/groomer-day productivity is holding (Port up to $526 vs $491, Hicksville flat at $413), meaning the team executes when present. The issue is scheduling depth.\n\nToday: Port has 5 groomers and 7.0 bather hours; Hicksville has 0 groomers and 0 bather hours. Next 7 days: 39 combined groomer-days (34 Port, 5 Hicksville) with 43 bather hours (all Port). Hicksville's coverage is dangerously thin."
